WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.654
Marcos built the ticket classifier's first eval in one afternoon.

00:00:05.954 --> 00:00:11.609
He asked the model itself to generate a hundred sample tickets, each with the right

00:00:11.609 --> 00:00:12.018
queue.

00:00:12.668 --> 00:00:15.216
The eval scored ninety-six percent.

00:00:15.516 --> 00:00:20.411
And in production, the support team complained about misrouted tickets every single

00:00:20.411 --> 00:00:20.647
day.

00:00:21.297 --> 00:00:23.835
When he looked at real tickets, he understood.

00:00:24.135 --> 00:00:26.969
Real customers write with typos everywhere.

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:28.738
They mix two topics.

00:00:29.038 --> 00:00:32.077
They paste screenshots, and reply to old emails.

00:00:32.377 --> 00:00:36.764
The hundred generated tickets were clean, short, and always about one thing.

00:00:37.064 --> 00:00:40.311
The eval measured a world that didn't exist.

00:00:40.961 --> 00:00:42.646
And there was a subtler problem.

00:00:42.946 --> 00:00:46.626
The same model that wrote the tickets also classified them.

00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:51.389
It wrote them the way it understood them, so it got almost all of them right.

00:00:51.689 --> 00:00:53.395
The eval was a mirror.

00:00:54.045 --> 00:00:54.853
Worse still.

00:00:55.153 --> 00:01:00.397
When a real ticket landed in the wrong queue, someone fixed the prompt, but the case

00:01:00.397 --> 00:01:02.145
never made it into the eval.

00:01:02.445 --> 00:01:06.767
Three months later, the same error came back with another prompt change.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:12.356
In this lesson, you'll learn where the cases for a trustworthy eval come from, and

00:01:12.356 --> 00:01:14.872
how to keep them trustworthy over time.

00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:19.957
The answer is to treat the dataset as a product asset.

00:01:20.257 --> 00:01:23.753
With an owner, versions, and rules for what gets in.

00:01:24.403 --> 00:01:25.543
Real origin.

00:01:25.843 --> 00:01:28.912
Cases come from production, anonymized.

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:33.872
Synthetic cases only come in to cover known gaps, and they stay labeled as

00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:34.502
synthetic.

00:01:35.152 --> 00:01:36.582
Reviewed labels.

00:01:36.882 --> 00:01:40.623
The expected answer comes from someone who knows the domain.

00:01:40.923 --> 00:01:43.240
And hard cases get a second reviewer.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.579
Coverage per category.

00:01:45.879 --> 00:01:51.185
Every category has cases, including the rare ones, and hard cases on purpose.

00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:53.132
Growth by bug.

00:01:53.432 --> 00:01:57.326
Every production error becomes a case, before it's fixed.

00:01:57.976 --> 00:01:59.281
And separation.

00:01:59.581 --> 00:02:02.111
Part of the dataset is for development.

00:02:02.411 --> 00:02:07.067
Another part is held out, only for the final decision, so you don't overfit.

00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:11.352
Of the five, growth by bug pays off the most over time.

00:02:11.652 --> 00:02:16.647
A dataset that only gets cases on the day it was created ages along with the

00:02:16.647 --> 00:02:17.173
product.

00:02:17.473 --> 00:02:21.901
One that receives every production error becomes the team's memory.

00:02:22.201 --> 00:02:25.710
Everything that broke once stays watched forever.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.814
And the order matters.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:32.184
Record the case, run the eval, and watch the case fail.

00:02:32.484 --> 00:02:33.708
Only then fix it.

00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:38.601
If the case doesn't fail before the fix, it doesn't reproduce the bug.

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:40.772
And the test protects nothing.

00:02:41.972 --> 00:02:43.215
Here's the flow.

00:02:43.515 --> 00:02:45.708
New cases come from two places.

00:02:46.008 --> 00:02:49.247
Samples of production traffic, and reported bugs.

00:02:49.897 --> 00:02:53.753
Personal data is removed or masked, before anything else.

00:02:54.403 --> 00:02:57.201
A domain expert labels the expected answer.

00:02:57.501 --> 00:02:59.947
Ambiguous cases get a second review.

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:05.792
The dataset is versioned like code, with a history of who added what, and why.

00:03:06.442 --> 00:03:08.769
One split is for iterating on the prompt.

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:12.242
The other is used only for the deploy decision.

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:16.366
And the eval harness runs the system against all of it.

00:03:17.566 --> 00:03:20.517
Let's follow a bug all the way into the dataset.

00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:23.003
On a Monday, support flags a ticket.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:28.319
I paid the bill, but order forty-five twenty-one still shows pending, and I want to

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:29.286
change the size.

00:03:29.586 --> 00:03:31.121
It went to the orders queue.

00:03:31.421 --> 00:03:33.598
Billing only saw it two days later.

00:03:34.248 --> 00:03:39.397
The engineer on call finds the ticket's trace, and sends it to the dataset with one

00:03:39.397 --> 00:03:39.770
click.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.880
It lands in the labeling queue, already masked.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.332
The labeler applies the guide.

00:03:45.632 --> 00:03:49.340
Two topics, and the payment blocks the order, so it's billing.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.884
Since it's a hard case, a second person confirms.

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:57.799
The case goes in, marked as coming from a bug, with the reason.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:01.751
The eval runs on the current prompt, and the new case fails.

00:04:02.051 --> 00:04:04.135
Expected billing, got orders.

00:04:04.435 --> 00:04:05.041
Good.

00:04:05.341 --> 00:04:07.514
Now it reproduces the bug.

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:13.107
Then the prompt gets the blocking topic rule, with an example that is not this

00:04:13.107 --> 00:04:13.551
ticket.

00:04:14.201 --> 00:04:15.996
The eval runs again.

00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:20.623
The case passes, and you check that nothing that used to pass now fails.

00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:22.974
Only then is the bug closed.

00:04:23.274 --> 00:04:28.460
And six months from now, when someone rewrites the prompt for something else, that

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:32.192
case is still there, and it speaks up if the error returns.

00:04:32.842 --> 00:04:36.310
And that labeling guide is what makes two labelers agree.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.992
It's a short document that answers the questions that will come up.

00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:41.528
Two topics?

00:04:41.828 --> 00:04:44.776
Use the queue of the one that blocks the customer.

00:04:45.076 --> 00:04:46.320
Only a screenshot?

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.034
Label by its content.

00:04:48.334 --> 00:04:49.073
Unsure?

00:04:49.373 --> 00:04:52.189
List both, and send it to a second review.

00:04:52.489 --> 00:04:56.872
Every new tie found in review becomes a rule in the guide.

00:04:57.172 --> 00:05:01.711
And the guide is versioned with the dataset, because labels and rules travel

00:05:01.711 --> 00:05:02.248
together.

00:05:03.448 --> 00:05:04.783
Here's one for you.

00:05:05.083 --> 00:05:07.861
The legal queue is two percent of tickets.

00:05:08.161 --> 00:05:12.937
Your random sample of two hundred cases brought four of them, and the eval shows

00:05:12.937 --> 00:05:15.027
seventy-five percent on that queue.

00:05:15.327 --> 00:05:16.490
What do you conclude?

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.909
That with four cases, the number says almost nothing.

00:05:23.209 --> 00:05:25.641
One error is worth twenty-five points.

00:05:25.941 --> 00:05:29.229
A single odd ticket explains the seventy-five percent.

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:31.488
Pull more cases from that queue.

00:05:32.138 --> 00:05:36.255
And don't ignore it because two percent barely moves the average.

00:05:36.555 --> 00:05:40.117
Weighing little in the average doesn't mean costing little.

00:05:40.417 --> 00:05:43.584
A legal ticket in the wrong queue can become a lawsuit.

00:05:43.884 --> 00:05:47.430
Rare, expensive categories deserve their own coverage.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.900
That's why the sample is stratified.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.411
You deliberately pull more cases from rare categories, until each one has a few

00:05:56.411 --> 00:05:56.807
dozen.

00:05:57.107 --> 00:06:02.138
In a category with twenty cases, each error moves the score by five points.

00:06:02.438 --> 00:06:04.606
In one with four, by twenty-five.

00:06:05.256 --> 00:06:09.277
The side effect is that the plain average no longer reflects production.

00:06:09.577 --> 00:06:11.895
So report each category on its own.

00:06:12.195 --> 00:06:16.419
And compute the overall score weighted by each category's real frequency.

00:06:17.069 --> 00:06:21.448
And before celebrating a two point gain, look at which cases changed.

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:24.978
In a hundred cases, two points are two tickets.

00:06:26.178 --> 00:06:28.407
So is synthetic data forbidden?

00:06:28.707 --> 00:06:29.451
No.

00:06:29.751 --> 00:06:34.233
Say your team wants to double the eval this week, and someone suggests asking the

00:06:34.233 --> 00:06:38.218
production model to generate three hundred new tickets, already labeled.

00:06:38.518 --> 00:06:41.005
Volume doesn't make up for origin.

00:06:41.305 --> 00:06:45.618
Cases generated by the model you're evaluating tend to be easy for it.

00:06:45.918 --> 00:06:48.193
The eval ends up measuring itself.

00:06:48.843 --> 00:06:50.972
But a total ban goes too far.

00:06:51.272 --> 00:06:55.518
Without synthetic cases, a rare category may have none at all.

00:06:55.818 --> 00:06:57.900
Use them to fill known gaps.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:04.029
Take hard real cases and generate variations, with typos, two topics, another

00:07:04.029 --> 00:07:04.711
language.

00:07:05.011 --> 00:07:08.155
Mark them as synthetic, and review the labels.

00:07:09.355 --> 00:07:10.508
Now the traps.

00:07:10.808 --> 00:07:12.587
The prompt's author labels alone.

00:07:12.887 --> 00:07:16.006
They tend to label the way the prompt already answers.

00:07:16.306 --> 00:07:18.658
Bring in someone from support or billing.

00:07:19.308 --> 00:07:21.752
Deleting the case that always fails.

00:07:22.052 --> 00:07:25.291
If a case fails on every version, review the label.

00:07:25.591 --> 00:07:30.457
If the label is right, that case is exactly what the dataset needs.

00:07:31.107 --> 00:07:32.565
Peeking at the holdout.

00:07:32.865 --> 00:07:37.988
Checking it on every iteration, and keeping the version that did best on it, turns

00:07:37.988 --> 00:07:39.550
it into development data.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.634
And copying eval cases into the prompt as examples.

00:07:43.934 --> 00:07:48.780
The eval passes from memory, and the score rises without production improving.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.142
In production, make it easy.

00:07:52.442 --> 00:07:56.790
A button or a command that sends a trace straight to the labeling queue.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.663
A dashboard with the dataset's size per category, and the date of each category's

00:08:02.663 --> 00:08:03.437
latest case.

00:08:04.087 --> 00:08:09.179
An alert when a new category shows up in production with no cases in the dataset.

00:08:09.829 --> 00:08:13.104
And a labeling queue with an owner and a deadline.

00:08:13.404 --> 00:08:16.867
A queue with no owner becomes a graveyard of traces.

00:08:17.517 --> 00:08:20.253
And remember the dataset holds real data.

00:08:20.553 --> 00:08:23.983
Masking only ID numbers and emails isn't enough.

00:08:24.283 --> 00:08:29.466
Tickets carry names, addresses, phone numbers, card numbers, and sometimes health

00:08:29.466 --> 00:08:29.786
data.

00:08:30.086 --> 00:08:35.697
Run a fuller detection pass, review a sample by hand on every batch, and restrict

00:08:35.697 --> 00:08:36.944
who can access it.

00:08:38.144 --> 00:08:40.129
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:40.429 --> 00:08:43.742
An eval is only as honest as its cases.

00:08:44.042 --> 00:08:48.002
Take them from production, and label them with people who know the domain.

00:08:48.302 --> 00:08:53.428
Cover the rare categories on purpose, keep a slice you never peek at, and turn every

00:08:53.428 --> 00:08:55.502
bug into a case before you fix it.

00:08:55.802 --> 00:08:57.917
That file becomes your team's memory.

00:08:58.567 --> 00:09:04.193
In the next lesson, we put these evals to work on a smell almost every team has, and

00:09:04.193 --> 00:09:09.015
almost nobody notices until month-end: text passed between two machines.

00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:10.520
Free-text handoff.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:11.649
See you there.
